---
// Figure: one hot key expiring under load, with no protection.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Slider from '../shared/Slider.astro';
import Readout from '../shared/Readout.astro';
import './stampedes.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="st-stampede" title="One popular key expires" advance={1000} legend={['client', 'hit', 'miss', 'write']}
  caption="Illustrative model, slowed down to watch: one key, read by four application processes, cached for 5 seconds. A rebuild takes 1 second from the miss to the new copy being stored (the query is the long trip to the database). Requests arrive at random at the chosen rate. The database has no limit on concurrent queries here.">
  <Slider slot="params" name="rate" label="Requests a second for the key" min={2} max={40} step={1} value={15} />
  <Fragment slot="stats">
    <Readout name="queries" label="Database queries, latest expiry" kind="database" />
    <Readout name="predicted" label="Requests a second × 1 s rebuild" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>While the copy is fresh, every request is a hit and nothing reaches the database. When the copy expires, every request that arrives in the following second misses, and each one sends its own query to the database. The first query's result is stored after one second; requests after that hit again. The number of queries per expiry follows the request rate: about 5 at 5 requests a second, about 15 at 15, about 40 at 40, varying from one expiry to the next because arrivals are random. Each of those queries returns the same answer.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { mountHotKey } from './hotkey-scene.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="st-stampede"]').forEach(root => mountHotKey(root, { mode: 'none' }));
</script>
